From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:38:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] JIT: prepare call forms ahead of their calls; a frozen kernel
 cache refuses the rest

tilelang.prepare(calls) compiles lazy-mode JIT functions' call forms in one
thread pool (lowering releases the GIL and nvcc is a subprocess) and keeps
each kernel under the key __call__ computes for the same call, so the call is
a cache hit. Calls are (function, args, kwargs) exactly as the call site
passes them; progress(done, total) reports as kernels finish.
JITImpl.prepare(calls) is the same for one function.

tilelang.cache.freeze(reason) refuses every kernel not already in memory: a
request that would load from the disk cache or compile raises
KernelNotPreparedError, naming the kernel and, through JITImpl, the call. A
program that runs on several processes prepares its kernels, then freezes, so a
missing kernel fails by name in every process alike, whatever each process's
disk cache holds, instead of compiling at an arbitrary point while the other
processes wait. thaw() undoes it.
Upstream-base: tile-ai/tilelang@2123e412ce2217b324c50c4ec967213906456ac5
Rationale: Keep ahead-of-time call preparation and fail-closed in-memory cache freezing.
Quality: A distributed rank must reject an unprepared kernel rather than compile while peers wait; thaw remains available.
Tests: retained upstream regression tests and local source checks; see
experiments/2026-11-04-tilelang-refresh/README.md for commands and results.
Historical GPU results in the original message do not qualify this rebase.
Upstream-status: Local patch; no upstream pull request as of 2026-11-04.

